    "contentMarkdown": "# ResearchOps\n\nResearchOps (операційне забезпечення досліджень) — це система координації спеціалістів, процесів, інструментів і ресурсів, яка забезпечує ефективне проведення досліджень у компанії. Вона допомагає командам рекрутувати учасників, адмініструвати дослідницькі артефакти, координувати інтерв'ю та тестування, а також робити аналітичні інсайти доступними для прийняття продуктових рішень.\n\n## What does ResearchOps cover?\n\nThe exact responsibilities vary with the organization and its research program.\n\n| Area | Typical operational work |\n|---|---|\n| Participants | Recruitment processes, scheduling, communication, and incentives |\n| Consent and data | Approved handling, access, retention, and participant requests |\n| Tools | Access, setup, evaluation, and support |\n| Knowledge | Findable evidence, research records, and clear source context |\n| Coordination | Shared calendars, support, and repeatable practices |\n| Capability | Onboarding and support for people conducting research |\n\nThe ResearchOps Community describes a broad framework for supporting research across organizations. A small team can apply the relevant practices without creating a separate department.\n\n## Example: repeated recruitment delays\n\nSuppose several product teams repeatedly struggle to recruit the same type of participant. ResearchOps can help establish a shared recruitment process, clear eligibility criteria, scheduling support, and a record of prior participation.\n\nResearchers still decide which participants fit each question and which method is appropriate. A reusable process should not quietly narrow every study to the easiest people to reach.\n\nReview who is missing from the recruitment approach, including people with relevant access needs.\n\n## ResearchOps vs. DesignOps vs. research\n\n[DesignOps](/ua/glossary/designops.html) supports the broader operation of design work. ResearchOps focuses on the conditions and infrastructure that make research possible and useful.\n\nConducting research includes defining questions, selecting methods, collecting evidence, and interpreting findings. Operational support enables that work; it does not replace the research judgment involved.\n\nResponsibilities may overlap, and one person may perform several roles. Make ownership clear enough that a team knows where to get help.\n\n## What changes when research tools use AI?\n\nReview what material the tool receives, how it is handled, and how generated interpretations remain traceable to evidence. Maintain a distinction between participant statements and AI-produced summaries.\n\nAn automatically generated research repository still needs appropriate access, source references, and maintenance. Searchable findings can be misleading if they omit the study's date, sample, context, or limitations.\n\nStart with a recurring bottleneck and evaluate whether the operational change improves research quality and access as well as reducing coordination effort.\n\n## Пов'язані терміни\n\n- [DesignOps](/ua/glossary/designops.html)\n- [AI in UX Research](/ua/glossary/ai-ux-research.html)\n- [Synthetic User Testing](/ua/glossary/synthetic-user-testing.html)\n- [Product Discovery](/ua/glossary/product-discovery.html)\n\n## Джерела\n\n- [About ResearchOps, ResearchOps Community](https://researchops.community/about/)\n- [ResearchOps 101, NN/g](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/research-ops-101/)\n- [Getting informed consent for user research, GOV.UK](https://www.gov.uk/service-manual/user-research/getting-users-consent-for-research)",
